= DFT-Machine-Learning (DFTML)

ML models for predicting band gap of 2D material from elemental
properties (those deriving from the chemical formula only)

== Setup

=== Prepare virtual environment

[source,bash]
----
# Make venv called ".venv" (with Python)
python -m venv .venv

# Make venv called ".venv" (with virtualenv)
virtualenv .venv

# Activate venv (Linux/WSL)
source .venv/bin/activate

# Activate venv (Windows)
.venv\Scripts\activate

# Install core packages and project in editable mode
pip install -e .
----

=== Upgrade packages

[source,bash]
----
git pull

# Install dev packages (1st time only)
pip install -e .[dev]

# Auto upgrade packages
pip-review -aC
----

== Running executables

Programs are the workhorse of this codebase. There are 2 kinds:
executables and helpers. Executables can be ran by the user via the CLI
with `python -m [name]` while helpers provide data for
executables. DO NOT RUN HELPERS ON THEIR OWN. Here is the list of
executables:

* `data.aggregate_c2db`
* `data.feat_correl`
* `data.feat_dist`
* `stats.one`
* `stats.many`

All executables support the `-h`/`--help` flags which prints
instructions on how to run them.

=== Asynchronous execution

Sometimes you need to run code on a remote server

[source,bash]
----
# Create tmux session called "dftml" (1st time only)
tmux new-session -t dftml

# Attach that session (to check logs)
tmux a -t dftml

# Run code here
# Press "Ctrl-B" THEN "d" to detach session

# You can now turn of your local machine and disconnect
----

=== A note on randomness

Random state is fixed to seed 67 for reproducibility on programs depend
on randomness, put `RANDOM=1` in front of the run command to be truly
random

=== Running models

The `stats.one` and `stats.many` executables are the current ways to run
a model. They both require knowing how to “name” models as shown below:

[cols=",",options="header",]
|===
|Model code |Meaning
|rf |Random forest
|xgb |eXtreme Gradient Boosting
|mlp |Multi-Layer perceptron
|gbt |Gradient-boosted tree
|hgbt |Histogram-based GBT
|adbt |AdaBoost-ed decision tree
|abet |AdaBoost-ed extremely randomized (eXtRa) tree
|ets |eXtRa trees
|===

[cols=",",options="header",]
|===
|Mode code |Meaning
|k |K-fold (k = 5)
|t |Tune (Bayesian)
|w |Weight (Linear regression)
|wt |Weight & tune
|===

==== Model name examples:

[source,bash]
----
# Random forest with k-fold
rf_k

# eXtreme Gradient Boosting with multi-layer perceptron, weighted
rf+mlp_w
mlp+rf_w
----

== Get files from ASDRP

Because ASDRP server setup is weird where when user ssh to it:

* They first get connected to `user@[master ip]`
* Then a `.bash_profile` from `user@master` run
`ssh user@[slave_ip]` to connect master to slave (on login)
* There's a double-password, caused by a transitive ssh connection
(local -> master -> slave)

Let’s define paths:

* src = source file on slave
* dst1 = destination on master
* dst2 = destination on local

[source,bash]
----
# First, if you're in slave, get out
exit

# To get files from slave to local, first rsync it to the master. From master:
rsync -aP user@[slave ip]:[src] [dst1]

# Then from local, run:
rsync -aP user@[master ip]:[dst1] [dst2]
----

If you are working in master, then just do the last step. Also, to avoid redoing the verification process, you should setup

== Explanations

=== Tuning

Joint-tuning is when we:

* Make an ensemble with all our models
* Grab the hyperparameter search space for it
* Tune the whole ensemble with those hyperparameters

Individual-tuning is when we:

* For each model, we:
** Grab the hyperparameter search space
** Tune the model with those hyperparameters
* Put all the tuned models into an ensemble

Theoretically, joint-tuning is better than inidividual-tuning because
the “team chemistry” argument where one ensemble can cover the weakness
of another. In this case, imagine one consistently underestimate, while
another consistent overestimates such that an appropriate weight will
cancel them out and give a good estimate. Individual-tuning is like
creating an “all-star” team that can make the same mistake (on
difficult/edge cases).

Practically, joint-tuning fails because of dimensionality. If we have 4
models with 5 hyperparameters each, individual tuning requires solving
four separate 5-dimensional optimization problems (manageable).
Joint-tuning combines them into a single 20-dimensional problem. The
probability of tuning and finding a decent dip in the loss function
(input: n-dimension-hyperparameters, output: squared error) is very low,
so very likely, we would land somewhere mediocre instead of that perfect
cancellation dip.

Individual-tuning, does not increase the dimensionality of the search
space so tuning will be more likely to find a very good dip in loss
function for each model (because of the reduced search space), which
correlate to maybe a decent dip in the ensemble loss function when
putting them toghether. Individual-tuning usually yields a better
real-world ensemble.

=== Learning curve

A learning curve plots the model’s error (or performance) vs the
training set size. It answers the question: “Will adding more data
improve the model?” We use error in our case, so lower is better as
opposed to using performance where higher is better

[width="97%",cols="15%,18%,16%,51%",options="header",]
|===
|Scenario |Training error (bias) |Testing error (variance) |Meaning:
Model…
|Overfit |Very Low |High |Captured noise instead of physics (too
complex).

|Underfit |High |High |Can’t capture the underlying pattern (too simple)

|Good Fit |Low (acceptable) |Low |Captured the underlying pattern while
being robust to noise (good complexity)
|===

=== Feature importance

There are two kinds of feature importance that we can use:
impurity-based (MDI) and permutation. MDI importance calculates
importance based on how much a feature reduces impurity (like Gini
impurity or entropy) during tree building. Some problems with MDI
include:

* Only for tree-based models
* Based on training-data because it was constructed during tree building
* Biased (makes a feature more important than it actually is) towards
high-cardinality (many unique values) feature because trees naturally
split on more unique values to reduce impurity

Permutation importance solves all of these problem by measuring change
in error on the test set when one feature (its rows in the dataset) is
shuffled. How much error increase will tell how important that feature
is. If error decreases or barely increases, that feature should be
eliminated.

However, highly correlated features can produce weird permutation
importance: they can appear both unimportant or important. One of those
feature should be eliminated.

== Developer notes

* For data aggregation, the data will always be in a Parquet, where the
first column is the target variable and the rest are features.
* Output files from programs must be directly embeddable into LaTex:
** Plot: `.pgf` (auxiliary PNGs are ok)
** Table: `.tex`
* Any file that depends on a random process must import `seeder` to
ensure reproducibility.
* To generate correct help messages, program names for Argparse should
follow `python -m [module name]` such as
`python -m data.feat_correl`
* `VotingRegressor`’s weights can be reset with `set_params` without
refitting (see test.py)
* We are only reporting performance of model types, not creating a super
good model, so use k-fold for base performance
* Takes way too long (removed):
** XGB with `dart` booster
** SVR
* XGB doesn’t like that feature (column) names has `[`, `]` or
`++<++` so we must change it to parenthesis
* Our model are always assumed to be a `Pipeline` with a first step of
`("scaler", RobustScaler())` and the second of a `("", VotingRegressor)`
whether it’s an ensemble or not
